import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int numDecodings(String s) {
        int[] memo = new int[s.length()];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1); // -1 indicates uncomputed index
        return decode(0, s, memo);
    }

    private int decode(int i, String s, int[] memo) {
        // Base case: Reached the end of the string, found 1 valid path
        if (i == s.length()) {
            return 1;
        }

        // Leading zeros cannot be decoded
        if (s.charAt(i) == '0') {
            return 0;
        }

        // Return cached result if already computed
        if (memo[i] != -1) {
            return memo[i];
        }

        // Option 1: Take 1 digit
        int ways = decode(i + 1, s, memo);

        // Option 2: Take 2 digits (if valid, i.e., between 10 and 26)
        if (i + 1 < s.length()) {
            int twoDigit = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i, i + 2));
            if (twoDigit >= 10 && twoDigit <= 26) {
                ways += decode(i + 2, s, memo);
            }
        }

        // Cache and return the result
        return memo[i] = ways;
    }
}

//CODE END

Time Complexity: O(N)
Explanation: There are N subproblems, corresponding to each index from 0 to N-1. Due to memoization, each index is computed at most once. Inside each recursive step, string slicing and parsing take O(1) time since the substring length is fixed at 2.

Space Complexity: O(N)
Explanation:
- O(N) memory is allocated for the memoization array (`memo`) of size N to cache results.
- O(N) memory is required for the implicit recursion call stack in the worst case (e.g., a string of all '1's without branching terminates at depth N).
